WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.510 --> 00:00:05.840
You can't make an omelette without breakin
a few eggs. And you can't become the number

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.990
one drama on television without generating
at least a little bit of offscreen drama.

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:14.540
That's certainly the case with NCIS. The hit
procedural might be popular now, but it wasn't

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:18.390
an easy path. What went on behind the scenes
to make this one of the highest rated shows

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:22.320
on television? That's a question worthy of
its own investigation.

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:24.260
Title troubles

00:00:24.260 --> 00:00:28.660
Few people remember it now, but when NCIS
first spun off from its parent show JAG in

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:34.340
2003, it was far from a guaranteed success.
In fact, CBS was worried that the show would

00:00:34.340 --> 00:00:40.559
be overshadowed by the network's runaway smash, CSI.
So, CBS called the show Navy NCIS for the

00:00:40.559 --> 00:00:45.129
first season in order to minimize confusion
over the similar acronyms. Showrunner and

00:00:45.129 --> 00:00:48.999
creator Donald Bellisario eventually convinced
the network to drop the "Navy" part because

00:00:48.999 --> 00:00:54.359
it's totally redundant. The "N" in NCIS already
stands for "Naval." It’s a lot to keep track

00:00:54.359 --> 00:00:54.919
of:

00:00:54.920 --> 00:00:55.760
"Federal agents don't move!"

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.260
"LAPD!"

00:00:56.260 --> 00:00:57.080
"NSA!"

00:00:57.080 --> 00:00:57.900
"NCIS!"

00:00:57.900 --> 00:00:58.880
"MOUSE!"

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.010
Mark Harmon’s rage

00:01:03.010 --> 00:01:08.860
Bellisario abruptly left NCIS in 2007 for
a very unexpected reason: Star Mark Harmon

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:13.030
forced him out. Reportedly, the two tangled
behind the scenes for months over Harmon's

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:17.080
dissatisfaction with Bellisario's "chaotic
management style." It led to a showdown, and

00:01:17.080 --> 00:01:21.070
the network decided they could live without
Bellisario, who was forced to resign. The

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:25.910
show continued with a presumably happy Harmon,
sitting pretty. Bellisario still got the last

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:27.440
word, however...

00:01:27.440 --> 00:01:30.160
NCIS: Lawsuit Angeles

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:34.560
Given the huge success of NCIS, it seems like
a no-brainer that it would eventually spawn

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:40.920
a spinoff. But the creation of NCIS: Los Angeles
in 2009 actually led to a major lawsuit. That's

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:45.030
because NCIS creator Donald Bellisario had
a clause in his contract giving him the right

00:01:45.030 --> 00:01:49.780
to create the first spinoff. After he was
forced off the show, CBS hired Shane Brennan

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:53.980
to create the offshoot show instead. After
years of legal wrestling, they settled out

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:59.550
of court for an undisclosed amount. At an
estimated net worth of $250 million, Bellisario

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.990
certainly isn’t suffering either way.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:03.740
NCIS: Red

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:10.170
In 2013, NCIS was poised to add yet another
series to its franchise in the form of NCIS:

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:15.739
Red. Introduced in a two-episode arc on NCIS:
Los Angeles, NCIS: Red was going to star John

00:02:15.739 --> 00:02:19.590
Corbett as the leader of Red Team, an anti-terrorist
unit within NCIS.

00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:22.510
"One NCIS team isn't enough"

00:02:22.510 --> 00:02:26.010
The network decided they didn't really dig
what they saw in the backdoor pilot and the

00:02:26.010 --> 00:02:31.510
series was canceled before it ever began.
Instead, the network launched NCIS: New Orleans.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:33.730
Don Johnson… almost

00:02:33.730 --> 00:02:38.010
Mark Harmon has been the face of NCIS for
well over a decade now. But viewers could

00:02:38.010 --> 00:02:41.560
have actually seen a very different face in
the role of Agent Leroy Gibbs. That's because

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.450
the role was actually offered to Don Johnson
first—and he turned it down. He wasn't the

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:50.239
only candidate, though. Rumor has it that
NCIS: New Orleans star Scott Bakula was also

00:02:50.239 --> 00:02:54.549
on the original shortlist for Agent Gibbs
way back in the day, since Bellisario and

00:02:54.549 --> 00:02:59.439
Bakula had previously worked together on Quantum
Leap. By the time NCIS: New Orleans came along,

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:03.999
the showrunners knew the right man for the
job was Bakula. Cause he's spectaculah.

00:03:06.959 --> 00:03:08.489
Pauley Perrette’s Twitter blunder

00:03:08.489 --> 00:03:14.700
In 2013, actress Cote de Pablo, who played
fan favorite NCIS agent Ziva David, abruptly

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.950
announced her departure from the show. It
was a surprise not only to fans, but to her

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:22.829
co-stars. Around the same time, Perrette posted
a tweet of herself wearing a shirt she’d

00:03:22.829 --> 00:03:28.200
received in 2007, which said "I love my job."
It seemed innocent enough, but it set off

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:32.709
a minor Twitter war with de Pablo’s fans,
who saw it as an attack. Allegedly, de Pablo

00:03:32.709 --> 00:03:36.419
called Perrette to ask that she stop tweeting
about the situation. Perrette shut down her

00:03:36.419 --> 00:03:40.139
feed for less than a day. She should have
just worn this shirt instead.

00:03:40.139 --> 00:03:41.519
Perrette's personal life

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:45.629
While Perrette has clearly been the subject
of real-world drama, she's done her best to

00:03:45.629 --> 00:03:51.139
turn lemons into lemonade by using her position
as a platform for advocacy. In 2006, she revealed

00:03:51.139 --> 00:03:55.340
that her former husband Francis “Coyote”
Shivers had subjected her to years of physical

00:03:55.340 --> 00:03:59.819
and emotional abuse. And just in 2015, after
she was randomly attacked by a mentally ill

00:03:59.819 --> 00:04:02.559
homeless man, she began championing the rights
of the homeless.

00:04:02.559 --> 00:04:07.559
"Just to be really super grateful to be alive,
but the other part is that, like, my heart

00:04:07.559 --> 00:04:13.529
breaks for that guy, and it breaks for every
single person that's out there on the street."

00:04:13.529 --> 00:04:17.100
She’s also stood up for marriage equality,
and even recorded music with a positive message

00:04:17.100 --> 00:04:18.940
for victims of abuse.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:20.980
Diane Neal’s Twitter meltdown

00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:26.290
In 2015, guest star Diane Neal launched an
angry Twitter attack on NCIS: New Orleans.

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:30.030
Upset over safety issues she encountered on
set while filming, Neal called the show’s

00:04:30.030 --> 00:04:34.720
production "dangerous and stupid." Saying
stuff like "they just use me like a rented

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:39.660
mule" and "these folks don't give a damn,"
didn’t go over well behind the scenes. Neal

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:44.170
apologized and deleted the tweets. Some sharp-eyed
folks on Reddit captured the drama and preserved

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:48.090
it for posterity, however. Moral of the story:
never tweet angry.

00:04:48.090 --> 00:04:50.920
A very important cameo

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.150
With three different shows and more than 20
combined seasons, the NCIS franchise has had

00:04:55.150 --> 00:04:59.410
a lot of high-profile guest stars. But none
of them can quite match the star power of

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:04.750
First Lady Michelle Obama, who appeared as
herself on the series in early 2016. Her appearance

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:09.420
was made in support of Joining Forces, a "nationwide
initiative supporting service members, veterans,

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:10.680
and their families."

00:05:10.680 --> 00:05:15.450
Kinda makes that time Taylor Swift showed
up on CSI seem pretty lame by comparison.

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:18.680
Thanks for watching! Subscribe to our YouTube
channel to watch more videos like the one

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:24.160
you just saw. And leave us a comment to let
us know what you’ve heard about NCIS...

